Week 1 Report

Linux Fundamentals, Linux Administration & Networking Basics

DevOps Internship | Davine Technologies

1. Linux Fundamentals
    Linux is a free, open-source, Unix-like operating system built around the Linux kernel. Distributions such
as Ubuntu, Debian and CentOS package the kernel with tools and software. Linux powers most servers,
cloud platforms and containers, which makes it the base skill for DevOps: Docker, Kubernetes, Jenkins
and Terraform all run on it. Key strengths are stability, security, scriptability and a rich command-line
interface.

2. Linux File System
    Linux uses a single tree starting at / (root), organised by the Filesystem Hierarchy Standard (FHS).
Everything, including devices and processes, is treated as a file.
Directory Purpose
/ Root of the file system
/home User home directories
/etc System configuration files
/var Variable data such as logs (/var/log)
/bin, /usr/bin Essential user commands
/tmp Temporary files
/opt Optional third-party software
/dev, /proc Devices and process information

3. Users & Groups
    Each user has a unique UID and belongs to one or more groups. Groups make it easy to give a team
shared access to resources. User data is stored in /etc/passwd, /etc/shadow and /etc/group.
sudo groupadd developers
sudo useradd -m -s /bin/bash user1
sudo usermod -aG developers user1
id user1
getent group developers

4. File Permissions
    Every file has permissions for the owner, the group and others: read (r=4), write (w=2) and execute
(x=1). For example, chmod 770 gives the owner and group full access and others none.
ls -l file.txt # -rwxrwx---
chmod 770 file.txt
chown user1:developers file.txt
chmod +x script.sh

5. Package Management
    Package managers install, update and remove software and handle dependencies. Ubuntu and Debian
use apt; RHEL and CentOS use yum/dnf.
sudo apt update
sudo apt upgrade -y
sudo apt install -y nginx
sudo apt remove nginx

6. Environment Variables
    Environment variables store configuration values used by the shell and applications, such as PATH,
HOME and custom values like APP_ENV. They keep configuration out of code and are widely used in
Docker, Jenkins and Kubernetes.
export APP_ENV=development
echo $APP_ENV
printenv
echo 'export APP_ENV=development' >> ~/.bashrc

7. Basic Networking
    Networking basics include IP addresses, hostnames, DNS, ports and protocols. Useful commands help
inspect and test connectivity.
Command Purpose
hostname / hostnamectl Show or set the system hostname
ip addr Display IP addresses and interfaces
ifconfig Legacy interface info (net-tools)
ping -c 4 google.com Test connectivity to a host

8. SSH
    SSH (Secure Shell) provides encrypted remote access to servers on port 22. In DevOps it is used to
manage servers, deploy applications, run Ansible playbooks and connect CI/CD tools to remote machines.
Key-based authentication is more secure than passwords.
ssh user@192.168.1.10
ssh-keygen -t ed25519
ssh-copy-id user@192.168.1.10

9. 20 Common Linux Commands
    # Command Description
1 pwd Show current directory
2 ls -la List all files with details
3 cd Change directory
4 mkdir -p Create directories
5 touch Create an empty file
6 cp -r Copy files or directories
7 mv Move or rename
8 rm -r Remove files or directories
9 cat Display file contents
10 grep Search text in files
11 chmod Change permissions
12 chown Change owner and group
13 useradd Create a user
14 groupadd Create a group
15 usermod -aG Add user to a group
16 apt update/upgrade Update packages
17 apt install Install software
18 ps aux List processes
19 top Live process monitor
20 kill Stop a process

10. Hands-on Activity Summary
    Created a DevOps directory with Projects, Scripts, Logs and Backup folders and added sample files.
Created the developers group with two users, applied group permissions with chgrp and chmod 770,
configured the hostname, verified connectivity with ping, checked the IP address, and compressed the
directory into DevOps.tar.gz.

11. Conclusion
    Week 1 built the Linux foundation needed for the rest of the internship: navigating the file system,
managing users and permissions, installing packages, monitoring processes, configuring networking and
connecting over SSH. These skills directly support Docker, Jenkins, Terraform and Kubernetes in the
coming weeks.